<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Chiang Mai</h1><p class="st-meta">1802 CE – 1899 CE · capital Chiang Mai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The restored Lan Na principality of Chiang Mai under Siamese overlordship, ruled by Kawila's descendants until absorbed into Siam's provinces.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-lampang">Lampang</a> helped expel the Burmese from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a>, he rebuilt the depopulated city of Chiang Mai from 1796, bringing in war captives from the Shan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> regions. In 1802 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom</a> recognised him as king of Chiang Mai, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila</a> and his successors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chet-ton-dynasty">Chet Ton dynasty</a> governed Chiang Mai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-lampang">Lampang</a> as related principalities, sending tribute and troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. Chiang Mai prospered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and teak, and in the late 19th century British firms from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a> operated large logging concessions, which led to disputes and Siamese intervention.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1870s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> placed commissioners in Chiang Mai, and the Chiang Mai treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> strengthened Siamese control. In 1899 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a> was turned into a regular monthon. The princely title lapsed with the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaew-nawarat">Kaew Nawarat</a> in 1939, and Chiang Mai is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand's</a> main northern city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-chiang-mai.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Chiang Mai" width="320" height="340"><figcaption>Image: NewFrontierHistoryEng · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lanna1883.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1802 CE – 1899 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chiang Mai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kawila, Inthawichayanon, Kaew Nawarat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Northern Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rattanatingsa, Chiang Mai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=kingdom-of-chiang-mai" aria-label="Kingdom of Chiang Mai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7359 1481 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Chiang Mai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7679 1737L7697 1683M7679 1737L7754 1922"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7697 1683h0M7754 1922h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7679 1737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7679 1737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chiang Mai. <a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=kingdom-of-chiang-mai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
